context github.hooks

uses github.events
uses github.activity
uses github.app
uses github.welcome
uses github.runs
uses github.metrics
uses github.pulls
uses github.releases
uses github.jobs
uses github.issues
uses github.maintainers
consumes bynk { Logger, Secrets, Clock, Fetch }
consumes bynk.crypto { Crypto }
consumes bynk.cloudflare { D1, Kv, RateLimit, EdgeCache }

---
Every delivery this service has accepted, one row each, keyed by GitHub's
delivery id. The key is what makes a redelivery harmless.
---
capability Deliveries = D1

---
Installation tokens, cached for `TOKEN_CACHE_SECONDS` so a burst of deliveries
mints one token, not one each. Keyed `installation:<id>`. A token here is a
credential: it expires within the hour and is never logged.
---
capability Tokens = Kv

---
The CI health page (#20, step 3): its template, with the stylesheet and script
it loads. Those two are content-hashed, so each deploy's page loads its own
and browsers may cache them for good.
---
embed dashboardTemplate = "embeds/dashboard.html"

asset dashboardCss = "assets/dashboard.css"

asset dashboardJs = "assets/dashboard.js"

---
The PR flow page (#26, step 4): its template and script. It shares the CI
page's stylesheet, so both read as one site.
---
embed prsTemplate = "embeds/prs.html"

asset prsJs = "assets/prs.js"

--- The releases page (#36, step 4): its template and script, with the same stylesheet.
embed releasesTemplate = "embeds/releases.html"

asset releasesJs = "assets/releases.js"

--- The issues page (#48, step 4): its template and script, with the same stylesheet.
embed issuesTemplate = "embeds/issues.html"

asset issuesJs = "assets/issues.js"

---
Signing maintainers in (#53): the script every page loads for the sign-in
link, and the page GitHub returns to.
---
asset signinJs = "assets/signin.js"

embed callbackTemplate = "embeds/callback.html"

---
The public metrics routes' per-address limit: 60 uncached requests a minute
is ample for a page polling every 30 seconds, and bounds what anyone can make
D1 read by varying the query string past the cache.
---
capability MetricsLimit = RateLimit @limit(60) @period(60.seconds)

---
The maintainers' figures' per-login limit (#53): they skip the edge cache, so
every request is a D1 read. 60 a minute is several signed-in tabs polling, and
bounds what one person's tabs (or a runaway one) can put on D1, where webhook
writes queue behind reads. Shared by every route `forMaintainer` guards.
---
capability MaintainerLimit = RateLimit @limit(60) @period(60.seconds)

---
Maintainers' actions' per-login limit (#53, step 5): each is a change on
GitHub, so far fewer than the figures' reads. 10 a minute is a burst of
re-runs after a bad night, and bounds a runaway page.
---
capability ActionLimit = RateLimit @limit(10) @period(60.seconds)

---
Cloudflare's edge cache for this Worker. Declaring it turns Workers Cache on,
so the public metrics routes' `@cache(scope: public)` answers are kept at the
edge for 30 seconds and served without running the Worker, and heavy viewing
doesn't become D1 reads. Every other response is sent `no-store`. A live
change purges the routes it affects (see `purgeFor`), so pages can refetch at once.
Every `@cache` route here is served `by v: Visitor`: a cached answer is
shared with every caller.
---
capability Edge = EdgeCache

---
The `bynk-lang` GitHub App. GitHub signs every delivery with HMAC-SHA256 of the
raw body under the App's webhook secret and sends it as
`X-Hub-Signature-256: sha256=<hex>`. The compiler generates the check: it runs
before the handler, compares in constant time, and answers `401` to anything
unsigned or forged.

GitHub signs no timestamp, so there is no replay window here. A replayed
delivery carries a delivery id we have already recorded, and is acknowledged
without being recorded twice.
---
actor GitHub { auth = Signature(secret = "APP_WEBHOOK_SECRET", header = "X-Hub-Signature-256") }

---
Who may read recorded activity: a caller presenting a JWT signed (HS256) with
`API_TOKEN_SECRET`. The compiler generates the check, including `exp` and
`nbf`, and answers `401` to a missing, forged or expired token.
`bynk token Reader --sub <name>` mints one. The token's `sub` names the reader, for logs.
---
actor Reader { auth = Bearer(secret = "API_TOKEN_SECRET"), identity = ReaderId }

---
A bynk-lang maintainer, signed in with GitHub (#53). The Worker issues the
token itself once GitHub has said who signed in and that they belong to the
organisation: an HS256 JWT signed with `MAINTAINER_TOKEN_SECRET`, whose `sub`
is their GitHub login. The compiler generates the check, including `exp` and
`nbf`. Whether they still belong to the organisation is checked again by the
routes, as a token outlives a membership. `bynk token Maintainer --sub
<login>` mints one for testing.
---
actor Maintainer { auth = Bearer(secret = "MAINTAINER_TOKEN_SECRET"), identity = MaintainerId }

---
A reader's name, from their token's `sub`.
---
type ReaderId = String where NonEmpty
